From 3241166321ab99819d05a28eee1235df9ef776e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: moto <j3tm0t0@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:16:46 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] BOOTWAIT (default on): answer NOT READY until the HDS is
 open; keep retrying the connection

The MSC log showed the Z reads the HDS boot area about 35 s after power-on
and serves everything after a soft reset from its Linux page cache. When
Wi-Fi/SMB was slower than that, the first imgdir/HDS read blocked the USB
task in vd_sync() (long enough for Linux's 30 s SCSI timeout), and if the
connection failed the HDS reads returned zeros as a successful read,
which then stayed cached until the Z was powered off.

- HDS reads/writes while the HDS isn't open return VD_NOTREADY. msc_disk.c
  keeps TinyUSB re-invoking the callback for up to 5 s (the USB task, HID
  and netpipe keep running), then fails the command with sense 02/04/01
  (becoming ready) via tud_msc_request_sense_cb instead of TinyUSB's
  02/3A/00 (medium not present). Linux sd retries 04/01 with a delay for
  about 3 minutes, and a failed read is never cached.
- SMB read/write errors on an open HDS -> 03/11/00 instead of zeros.
  Other -1 cases in the virtual FAT stay zero-filled successes as before.
- No vd_sync() for the image directory, ID 6 and netpipe writes.
- connect_task keeps retrying every 5 s after the first 5 tries and runs
  vd_mount() when SMB comes up. status shows smb=retrying and
  notready=/ioerr= counters; MSC_LOG marks defer/notready/ready-after.
- The Linux IMAGE directory write-back (W 0x4160) is accepted.

-DBOOTWAIT=OFF restores the old behaviour (binary identical).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PrGdt1XK1exBgERrMmLA1u
